// ===================================================== format ===============
//
// rt.pyFormat (jsrt.go:9682) and rt.pyFormatBody (jsrt.go:9737):
// [[fill]align][sign][0][width][.precision][type], which is the slice of the
// format mini-language an f-string replacement field in practice writes.
//
// What the Go twin does NOT implement, and therefore neither does this port:
//   - the ',' and '_' thousands separators. Neither is a digit, so the width
//     scan stops before it and `.` is not next, which leaves it sitting at the
//     TYPE position: `{n:,}` takes pyFormatBody's default branch and prints
//     pyString(n) with no grouping at all. Reproduced exactly.
//   - the 'e', 'E', 'g', 'G', '%', 'c', 'n' and 's' verbs, which likewise fall
//     into the default branch and print pyString / pyRepr.
//   - the '!a' conversion: only conv == "r" is distinguished, so "!s" and "!a"
//     both take the pyString path.
//   - '=' alignment does not put the padding after the sign the way CPython
//     does; the Go twin treats '=' as a synonym for '>'.

